@h1 The mental model h1@

Here is the single idea that makes Wit make sense: it is a stack of layers built
on top of plain prose. Each layer is optional. You climb only as high as a given
document needs — and most documents never leave the bottom two. Nothing above
prose costs you anything until you reach for it.

Once you see the five layers, you can predict how any feature behaves, because
every feature lives on one of them.

@h2 Layer 0 — Prose h2@

The ground floor, and where most of every document lives. You write ordinary
paragraphs; a blank line starts a new one. Two inline marks, @code *bold* code@
and @code _italic_ code@, are always available, and a line beginning with
@code ~ code@ is a comment that never appears in the output.

@@@pre
The keeper had not spoken aloud in *eleven days*.

He had not noticed, until his own _voice_ surprised him.
pre@@@

This layer alone is a complete, valid Wit document. If you write nothing but
prose forever, Wit is happy.

@h2 Layer 1 — Nodes h2@

When prose isn't enough, wrap a span of content in a named structure — a
@strong node strong@, written @@@code @name … name@ code@@@. The built-in
vocabulary (headings, lists, tables, figures, sectioning) needs no setup; you
just use it, and the renderer decides whether it's a block or an inline span.

@@@pre
@h2 A Quiet Morning h2@

@aside The lamp burned steady through the storm. aside@
pre@@@

Nodes are covered in full in
@a |href /docs/start/nodes/| Nodes and core vocabulary a@. This is the layer you
reach for most after prose.

@h2 Layer 2 — Definitions h2@

Name a chunk of content once, use it anywhere. A @strong definition strong@
starts with @code # code@ instead of @code @ code@. Recall it later with a bare
@@@code @name code@@@. The simplest form is a single line:

@@@pre
#epigraph: The sea does not forgive forgetting.

@epigraph
pre@@@

For longer content, open a value block and close it with @@@code !! code@@@:

@@@pre
#epigraph:
The sea does not forgive forgetting.
— a coastal proverb
!!
pre@@@

And to wrap a long manuscript section, use a block-form definition,
@@@code #name … name# code@@@, with @code ... code@ marking where the body goes.
Definitions can also capture parameters between @@@code ||…|| code@@@ and drop them
in with @@@code ::name:: code@@@:

@@@pre
#chapter ||number, title||
@h2 ::number:: — ::title:: h2@
...
chapter#

@chapter |number I| |title The Keeper|
For eleven days the lamp had burned untended.
chapter@
pre@@@

That builds a heading reading @strong I — The Keeper strong@ followed by the
prose you passed in. Definitions are how you make your own components; change one
in a single place and every use updates.

@h2 Layer 3 — Data h2@

Structured values, held apart from prose. A @strong record strong@ is
@@@code { key - value } code@@@; a @strong collection strong@ is
@@@code [ … ] code@@@, often of records. Pull a value into prose with dotted
access, @@@code @name.field code@@@:

@@@pre
#keeper: { name - Aldous Vane, years - 11 }

@keeper.name has tended the light for @keeper.years years.
pre@@@

That renders @strong Aldous Vane has tended the light for 11 years. strong@ You
write the fact once, as data, and reference it wherever it belongs.

@h2 Layer 4 — Logic h2@

The narrowest layer, and a genuine last resort. @strong Conditionals strong@,
@@@code (if …) … (end) code@@@, show content only when the data warrants it;
@strong iteration strong@, @@@code (each … as …) … (end) code@@@, builds content
@em from em@ a collection. For anything those can't express, a
@@@code <% … %> code@@@ script block is the single, explicit escape hatch.

@@@pre
#keeper: { years - 11 }

(if @keeper.years is 11)
@aside(class note) A milestone year. aside@
(end)
pre@@@

Wit is @strong not Turing-complete strong@, and that is on purpose. The logic
layer shapes a document from its data; it is not a programming language wearing a
document costume.

@h2 One document, climbing all five h2@

Put the layers together and each line lives on exactly one of them:

@@@pre
@h1 The Lamp Keeper h1@

#epigraph:
The sea does not forgive forgetting.
!!

#keeper: { name - Aldous Vane, years - 11 }

@epigraph

@keeper.name has tended the light for @keeper.years years.

(if @keeper.years is 11)
@aside(class note) A milestone year. aside@
(end)
pre@@@

Reading top to bottom: the heading and epigraph text are @strong prose (L0) strong@
inside @strong nodes (L1) strong@; the @@@code #epigraph code@@@ and
@@@code #keeper code@@@ lines are @strong definitions and data (L2, L3) strong@; the
@@@code @keeper.name code@@@ access pulls @strong data into prose (L3) strong@; and
the @@@code (if …) code@@@ is @strong logic (L4) strong@. The reader of the output
sees a clean document; you see a source that climbed only as far as it needed.

@h2 The governing rule h2@

Across every layer, one rule holds: @strong content goes in a node's body;
parameters are metadata strong@. Never carry the content of a node in a parameter.
Structure appears only where the author reaches for it — never by accident.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li @strong Expecting @@@code {{path}} code@@@ or @@@code ::name:: code@@@ to interpolate in prose. strong@ They don't. In ordinary prose, both are just text. @@@code ::name:: code@@@ is a capture hole that only works @em inside a definition body em@; @@@code {{path}} code@@@ is live @em only inside a @@@code @@ code@@@ raw-node body em@. To put data into prose, use @@@code @name.field code@@@. li@
@li @strong Reaching for operators that don't exist. strong@ Conditionals understand @code is code@ and @code equals code@ (they're synonyms) and a bare truthy check — and nothing else. There is no @code contains code@, no @code < code@ or @code > code@, no @code and code@ or @code or code@. Write @@@code (if @tags contains urgent) code@@@ and it silently misfires. Restructure to an @code is code@ check or a truthy flag. li@
@li @strong Climbing higher than the document needs. strong@ If a fact appears once, just type it. Data and logic earn their keep when something repeats or must assemble itself — not before. li@
ul@

@h2 See also h2@

The layer you'll use most is nodes —
@a |href /docs/start/nodes/| Nodes and core vocabulary a@ covers them in full. To
see all five layers cooperate in a real build, follow
@a |href /docs/start/tutorial/| the tutorial a@. When you're ready to pick a
direction, @a |href /docs/start/choose-your-path/| choose your path a@.
